Terraform (IAC) Scenario Based Interview 🟪 

🚨🚨 What Happens When Someone Manually Creates Infrastructure in Terraform-Managed AWS Environment?🚨🚨

📌  Let's Imagine

🧰  We have Scrupulously set up your AWS infrastructure using Terraform.
Everything is codified, version-controlled, and reviewed.

⚠️ ⚠️ Then… someone goes to the AWS Console and manually creates a new resource in that same environment. 🥲 

→ Do you have any idea what happens now ?? Make a guess 😇 

→ Ok! Lets deep dive what will happen in this scenario.

📦 1. Terraform is Blind to Resources It Didn't Create. It don't know about the resources in Cloud account 📦📦
Terraform manages only what's:

→ It only Knows What is defined an tracked in .tf state file.

→ When someone creates infra manually (say, an EC2 instance), it's not recorded in Terraform state.

✅ Terraform won’t touch it
❌ Terraform won’t manage it
🤐 Terraform doesn’t even know it exists.


🧪 2. Manual Resources May Cause Conflicts or Drift
Here’s where it gets risky:

➤ ❌ Conflicts
If someone manually creates an S3 bucket with the same name as one defined in Terraform, terraform apply will fail with a resource conflict.

➤ ⚠️ Drift
If someone manually modifies a Terraform-managed resource. Terraform detects it as drift — a mismatch between real infra and .tfstate.

🔍 3. How Terraform Reacts During terraform plan and apply

Scenario	                                                      Terraform Reaction
➤Manually created new resource	              ->  Ignored (not managed)
➤Manually modifies existing 
Terraform-managed resource                	      ->  Detected as drift
➤Terraform defines a resource
 that conflicts with manual one                       ->	 apply will fail

🛠️ 4. How to Handle This Situation 

➤ Import the Resource, So that Terraform will know the Created resource details under Terraform Hood.

# Terraform import aws_instance.kishore_instance i-0123456789abcdef0  

The above command will sync the resource into terraform state.

🚨 NOTE : Manually we need to write resource data in our configuration files.

✅ See as a infra team we taking care of so many things, But Sometimes application users they create due to urgency.
 
✅ I have solution for that, If your team follow IAC infra only then restrict the users to create resources through console using IAM POLICIES.


🧠 Final Verdict:
- Manual changes in a Terraform-managed AWS environment aren’t just bad practice but If IAC is managing INFRA there are several risks. ✳️ 

So Manual changes 

👉 They break visibility
👉 They cause drift
👉 They increase the chance of production issues

